    add('change-of-variables','Where does exact density come from?',F('volume')+M(r'\log p_X(x)=\log p_Z\!\left(f^{-1}(x)\right)+\log\left|\det J_{f^{-1}}(x)\right|',True),S,idea='Exact density requires a tractable inverse and Jacobian determinant.',provenance='ANALYTICAL / EXACT · inverse-Jacobian convention; 1D stretch illustration',source='Flow',notes='For x=2z a small interval doubles in length, so density halves: pX(x)=pZ(x/2)/2. The inverse derivative is 1/2, hence plus log(1/2), equivalent to minus log2. Keep normalizing flows distinct from Lecture II flow matching.',answer='Change of variables combines the base density with the inverse map’s local volume correction.')
    S='08 · Many small transformations?'
    add('four-answers','Four answers to the same question', '<div class="rows"><div><strong class="blue">Autoregressive</strong><span>Sample one part conditioned on earlier parts.</span></div><div><strong class="purple">VAE</strong><span>Sample a latent prior; decode with a probability model.</span></div><div><strong class="teal">GAN</strong><span>Learn a generator through adversarial feedback.</span></div><div><strong class="amber">Flow</strong><span>Invertibly transform a known density.</span></div></div>',S,idea='How do we learn to turn randomness into valid data?',provenance='CONCEPTUAL comparison · sampling access differs from density access',notes='Ask students to supply each mechanism before you say it. We have not ranked the families by universal sharpness or quality. No family automatically solves all coverage, likelihood and computation requirements.',answer='They differ in factorization, latent inference, training signal, or invertibility.')
